(3) feature9_lazy_relation_warning:
Here are the public test cases for feature9_lazy_relation_warning:
{
  "description": "When a distribution scope returns a query object that has not been executed yet, a warning is logged advising the caller to force execution inside the scope.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "program": [
          {
            "op": "scope",
            "options": {},
            "body": [
              {
                "op": "lazy_read"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "log=Call `to_a` inside block to execute query on replica\n"
    }
  ]
}

Pinned semantics: (1)+(2) the 'job' op carries a 'role' field, either read_only or read_write, and renders as job=replica — job reads never render as read=. Under by_default, the sticky write-pin does NOT survive between job invocations; context resets each run, so a read_write job still reads from the replica on every invocation. (3) the 'lazy_read' op inside a scope body emits only the warning line — no read= line, because the query was never executed; note the exact backticks around to_a and the single-space separators.

Two asks, two real files. Verbatim below.

(1) Scope-interaction variant = feature3_2_block_inside_default:
Here are the public test cases for feature3_2_block_inside_default:
{
  "description": "With default-distribution mode on, reads inside a distribution scope stay on the replica even after a write within the scope; once the scope exits, an earlier write pins the next read to the primary.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "by_default": true,
        "program": [
          {
            "op": "scope",
            "options": {},
            "body": [
              {
                "op": "read"
              },
              {
                "op": "write"
              },
              {
                "op": "read"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "op": "read"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "read=replica\nread=replica\nread=primary\n"
    }
  ]
}

(2) Primary-override variant = feature3_3_primary_override:
Here are the public test cases for feature3_3_primary_override:
{
  "description": "With default-distribution mode on, opening a scope that forces the primary routes its reads back to the primary.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "by_default": true,
        "program": [
          {
            "op": "scope",
            "options": {
              "primary": true
            },
            "body": [
              {
                "op": "read"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "read=primary\n"
    }
  ]
}

Pinned semantics: in (1) the sticky write-pin is scoped — a write INSIDE a distribution scope does not pin later reads within that same scope (both stay on the replica), but once the scope exits, a write that occurred before entry governs the next top-level read. That asymmetry is the trap; implement it exactly. In (2) 'primary: true' as a scope option overrides by_default distribution for that scope's reads entirely.
